2010-02-17 Tomas Kramar, version 0.1.0
  * initial release

2010-02-20 Tomas Kramar, version 0.1.1
  * XML special characters are properly escaped. If your template data
	contained '<', '>' or '&', the document generated by previous version would be
	invalid. The characters are now escaped to '&lt;', '&gt;' and '&apos;'
  * minor test refactoring

2010-03-30 Thomas Ritz
  * Support changes in the header
  * new matcher "contain_in"

2010-09-22 Tomas Kramar, version 0.2.0
  * ruby 1.9 support
  * use instance variables in templates

2010-11-3 Ed Hirgelt, version 0.2.1
  * do not remove whitespace when processing the template - this was causing
    problems

2026-07-10 version 0.2.3
  * performance: OdtEruby#convert is now O(n) instead of O(n^2) in the number of
    template nodes. It was appending each node's compiled lines with
    "buffer << arr; buffer.flatten!", rescanning the whole (growing) buffer on
    every node; switched to "buffer.concat(arr)". Large ODT bodies compile
    dramatically faster (e.g. ~37x at 10k nodes).
  * compatibility: works with rubyzip >= 1.0 (Zip::File). Requiring the gem
    previously failed on any modern setup because rubyzip removed the "zip/zip"
    require path and renamed Zip::ZipFile to Zip::File.
  * tests: run on RSpec 3 and modern Ruby.

2026-07-10 version 0.4.0
  * feature: meta.xml is now processed too, so template markup placed in the
    document metadata (e.g. a templated dc:title) is filled in like anywhere
    else. Implements the enhancement from #5 (thanks leavengood).

2026-07-10 version 0.3.0
  * feature: embedded objects (e.g. charts) placed inside a loop are now
    duplicated once per iteration, each evaluated against that iteration's
    binding. References in the draw:frame are rewritten to unique "Object N"
    directories, supporting files (meta.xml, ObjectReplacements) are copied and
    the new entries are registered in META-INF/manifest.xml. Ported from #18
    (thanks luizloja).
  * bugfix: String#escape_xml and #convert_newlines no longer raise
    FrozenError on Ruby 2.7+ when handed a frozen string. They now use
    non-mutating gsub instead of gsub!, so the substitution is always applied
    (previously the only proposed workaround silently skipped it).
